## Result files delivered so far
- `results/gap_drivers_synthetic.parquet` (`experiments/summarise_syn.py` from `syn_gap.jsonl`): synthetic arm, 504 rows = 4 sigma_theta (0, .01, .02, .04) x 3 item-config interaction strengths phi (.25, .5, 1) x 7 K (2..128) x 6 n (30..800). Computed on 4 simulated worlds per cell (N_pop = 30000, base accuracy .35), random search over K configurations, 150 draws of S per world; `gap` = reported minus exact theta of the selected configuration; `predicted` = search-log predictor E max Z (multiplier bootstrap, B = 200, equal-theta null) on the same S. `gap_lo/hi`, `pred_lo/hi` are t-intervals over the 4 worlds (`ci_kind = world-spread-t`); the within-world Monte-Carlo SD of a mean is `gap_sd_within/sqrt(150)`. Reading: the equal-theta predictor is within about 7% of the gap where the sampling noise dominates the true spread (median predicted/gap 0.93-0.95 at sigma_theta = 0, n <= 200), and it overshoots as n grows with sigma_theta > 0 (median 1.2-1.3 at n = 400, 1.6-1.75 at n = 800), as it should since it ignores true differences.
- `results/decision_rates_synthetic.parquet` (`summarise_decisions.py` from `decisions_synth.jsonl`; produced by `run_decisions.py --arm synthetic`): C4 on simulated worlds with equal true accuracy (`sigma0.0`, exact null) and true spread 0.01, 0.03; 6 worlds per scenario, 500 draws of S per world and cell, n in {60, 120, 240}, random search with 20 or 100 evaluated configurations out of 128, default = configuration 0. `rate` = mean over worlds of the rate over the 500 draws; `rate_lo/hi` = t-interval over the worlds (`ci_kind = world-spread-t`). Tuned-vs-untuned declares 'tuning helped' when (reported tuned - default) on the same S has one-sided z > 1.645 (`naive`), or beyond the max-t band (`maxt`), or on a held-out half (`split`). Under the exact null the naive rule declares in 32% (B = 20) and 54% (B = 100) of draws at n = 120, max-t in 1.6% / 4.5%, the split in 6%. For A-vs-B (tuned A against tuned B, two independent searches, `budget_ratio` = A's evaluations / B's) the reported difference favours the bigger-budget side in 74% of draws under the null (0.45 at equal budgets; ties count as not winning) while the one-sided z test rarely fires (2%): the bias is in the direction of the reported comparison, not in the test's size.
- `results/protocols_synthetic.parquet`, `results/bound_coverage_synthetic.parquet` (`summarise_protocols.py` from `protocols_synth.jsonl`; produced by `run_protocols.py --arm synthetic`): C5 and C6 on simulated worlds with true spread sigma_theta in {0, 0.01, 0.03}, 8 worlds each, 500 draws of the item set U per world and cell, equal total item budget N_tot in {60, 120, 240} for search and report together. Searchers: random with B in {20, 100} configurations evaluated (N_tot 60, 120, 240), hill-climb (120, 240), successive halving with 20 or 100 full-set-equivalent evaluations (120, 240); 128 configurations, truth = exact theta of the deployed configuration. Protocol rows: `overstate_prob` = P(reported > truth of the deployed configuration) (0.5 is the calibrated value, `nominal`); `bias`, `mae`, `reported`; `true_perf_cost` = truth of the protocol's deployed configuration minus truth of the one the naive protocol deploys (what holding items out costs in deployed quality); for bounds, `coverage` = P(lower bound <= truth) against `nominal` 0.95 and `width` = mean(truth - lower bound). `n_search`/`n_report` are the item counts (cross-fit: folds of 4/5 search, all items report; its estimand is the mean accuracy of the fold-trained configurations, not the deployed one). `candidate_set` = `evaluated` (configurations the search touched; for successive halving the last-round survivors only) or `declared` (all 128). `K_eff` is empty in these files (defined only for fitted real worlds). `coverage_cons` and `coverage_noisy` are the versions scored against truth plus fresh-item noise; in exact worlds they only differ through `coverage_noisy`. Intervals are t-intervals over the 8 worlds (`world-spread-t`); `n_boot_or_worlds` gives the count. Reading (N_tot = 120, sigma_theta = 0): the naive report exceeds the truth in 100% of draws (bias +0.105 at B = 100); the optimism bootstrap in 90% (+0.035), .632 in 97%, cross-fit 54%, splits 47-55% (bias < 0.01). max-t, Bonferroni and Sidak over the declared set cover at 96% (nominal 95%); over the evaluated set they cover at 95% for random B = 100 and hill-climb but at only 48-65% for successive halving, whose evaluated set is the last two survivors (the winner's curse acts before they are chosen); the pointwise bound covers 9% (random B = 100). The cost in deployed quality of splitting is below 0.01 in these worlds (sigma_theta = 0.03: -0.002 for a 20% report share, -0.007 for 50%).
- Real arm, common definitions (read the interval paragraph at the end of this bullet: `gap` and the C4 rates carry a wider interval than the others). Pool = 1500 MMLU-Pro items (`mmlu-pro-N1500-all`) x 128 configurations (`data/matrix.npz`), Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct. Truth is **accuracy on pool items outside S** (S = the items the search/report used); there is no exact theta. `ci_kind = pool-bootstrap`: the pool is resampled by items (R replicates, S drawn inside each); the interval is the bootstrap spread placed on the point estimate (resampled pools contain duplicated items, so their mean is not the pool's value), 95% percentiles. The point estimate is the pool itself (`rep = -1`) with more S draws. The Monte-Carlo variance of the S draws is removed from the spread where it can be resolved; where it cannot (between-pool spread not larger than the draw noise) the unshrunk spread is returned, an upper bound (`se_between` NaN / `ci_mc_limited = True`). The pool is 1500 items, so intervals describe this pool's sampling uncertainty, not another benchmark's. **Two intervals (corrected 2026-11-26, `total_ci`).** The between-pool variance is var(replicate estimates) minus the mean Monte-Carlo variance of their S draws, a difference of two nearly equal numbers at small n (n = 30: spread 0.0077, draw noise 0.0076), and the point estimate rests on a finite number of draws (2000 for `gap`, 500 for the C4 rates). `gap_lo/hi` (gap_vs_n, gap_drivers) and `rate_lo/hi` (C4) therefore add (i) two standard errors of the replicate variance to the between-pool variance and (ii) the Monte-Carlo SE of the point in quadrature. Columns: `gap_se_between` (point estimate of the between-pool SE, NaN where the subtraction is not positive), `gap_se_between_upper` (with the added term), `gap_mc_se_point`, `gap_between_resolved` (between-pool variance exceeds two SE of the replicate variance; False for most cells at n = 30-60, where the between-pool SE is not resolved and the interval is carried by the upper term), `ci_mc_limited` in C4 = the same, not resolved. Every other column (`gap_vs_pool`, `reported`, `fresh`, predictors, paired differences, C5/C6 rates) uses the total bootstrap spread without removing draw noise, or `pool_ci` as before, and is wider where draw noise dominates. Before this correction the n = 30 exhaustive `gap` interval was 0.1138-0.1183 (between-pool SE 0.0012); the corrected interval is 0.1082-0.1237. The narrow interval was an artefact of the variance subtraction plus the omitted point error, not a between-pool property.
- `results/gap_vs_n_real.parquet` (`summarise_gap.py` from `real_gap.jsonl`, group c1): gap of random search (5, 20, 100 configurations), exhaustive search (128), hill-climb and successive halving (20, 100) at n in {30, 60, 100, 200, 400}. Point: 2000 draws of S; R = 200 replicates x 100 draws. `gap` = reported minus accuracy on pool items outside S; `gap_vs_pool` = reported minus accuracy on the whole pool (S included), which has no finite-population shift. At n = 400, S is 27% of the pool: report `gap` (0.0257 exhaustive, CI 0.0136-0.0365) against `gap_vs_pool` (0.0189) and say that the outside-S truth is shifted by the item overlap (flag column). `noise_floor` = standard error of the fresh-item accuracy on the items outside S. `pred_kappa`, `pred_refined`, `keff` only for random B >= 20 and exhaustive. Measured: n = 30 exhaustive gap 0.116 (reported 0.393 vs fresh 0.277), n = 60 0.079, n = 100 0.060, n = 200 0.040. The search-log predictor `pred_kappa` over the gap is 0.95 at n = 30, 1.02 at 60, 1.07 at 100, 1.16 at 200, 1.26 at 400 (`pred_over_gap`): it matches where noise dominates and overshoots as n grows. `pred_refined` equals `pred_kappa` here because the empirical-Bayes estimate of sigma_theta from S is about 0 (S sample noise 0.03 against a spread of 0.014): the refined predictor gives no gain on the real matrix.
- `results/gap_drivers_real.parquet` (group c2): exhaustive search over K nominal in {8, 32, 128} candidate configurations (K = 128: all) x spread stratum `full` (random draw of K), `half` (configurations in the central half by pool accuracy), `tight` (densest window), n in {60, 120, 240}. Same draws and replicate scheme as above. `theta_noise_ratio` = pool sigma_theta of the stratum over sigma_bar/sqrt(n) (true spread over the standard error of one configuration on n items). This is the tolerance term that sets how well the equal-theta predictor tracks the gap: `pred_over_gap` is 0.93-1.01 in all `half` and `tight` cells (ratio 0.5-3.2) and in the `full` strata with ratio below 2.5, and 1.07-1.32 in the `full` strata with ratio above 3.5 (n = 60 K = 8, n = 120 K = 32 and 128, n = 240) (correlation of `pred_over_gap` with the ratio over the 21 cells 0.85). Wording for C2: the predictor is within about 5% of the gap when the spread is small relative to the noise, and overshoots by up to a third when it is not.
